  "title": "Persistent composition in affine chains is a question about row sequences",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "For chains of three rings with linear or affine rules, each ring its own, and affine contact laws (proposal parities or self-blind sums, chosen independently at each contact), let r_T(c) = e_c M^T be the support of contact c's value at lag T. (1) c1 binds rings A and B at lag T exactly when r_T(c1) meets both; c2 binds B and C exactly when r_T(c2) meets both; the chain composes at T exactly when r_T(c2) meets A and C. (2) Each sequence r_T(c) is eventually periodic, so whether both contacts persist and the chain composes at every lag from d + 1 on is decided exactly by iterating the two row vectors until each repeats. (3) For one finite chain that iteration is a complete proof of its verdicts.",
